WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
You cannot purify what you cannot detect.

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.420
A tumor in an embryo is a slow, messy detector.

3
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.680
So Levi-Montalcini moved the test into a dish.

4
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.840
She set a chick ganglion in a clot of blood plasma.

5
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.940
Then she placed a fragment of tumor near it, not touching.

6
00:00:24.883 --> 00:00:30.103
Within 24 hours, the ganglion put out a dense halo of fibers.

7
00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:33.163
The halo was thickest toward the tumor.

8
00:00:33.823 --> 00:00:36.623
The whole experiment now fit in a dish.

9
00:00:37.423 --> 00:00:39.443
That halo is a bioassay.

10
00:00:39.923 --> 00:00:46.443
It uses a living response as the measuring device, so it works before you know what the molecule is.

11
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:49.893
A bioassay can also be quantitative.

12
00:00:50.893 --> 00:00:52.913
Dilute a sample step by step.

13
00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:58.633
The highest dilution that still gives a halo tells you how much factor the sample

14
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:02.653
holds. Divide by the milligrams of protein in the sample.

15
00:01:03.093 --> 00:01:07.933
That gives the specific activity: the activity per milligram of protein.

16
00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:13.937
Now test a sample from each purification step with the same assay.

17
00:01:14.757 --> 00:01:17.177
Every step is judged by one question.

18
00:01:17.957 --> 00:01:20.457
Did the activity per milligram go up?

19
00:01:21.237 --> 00:01:25.257
The assay lets you follow the factor before you know what it is.

20
00:01:25.437 --> 00:01:29.737
So keep this rule: you cannot purify what you cannot detect.
